tent="citation_title=Investigating studentsâ€™ perceived satisfaction, behavioral intention, and effectiveness of English learning using augmented reality; citation_author=Chang, Y.J., Chen, C.H., Huang, W.T., Huang, W.S.; citation_publication_date=2011; citation_firstpage=1; citation_lastpage=6" data-hid="citation_reference-399300-55"><meta name="citation_reference" content="citation_title=Alien Contact!: Exploring teacher implementation of an augmented reality curricular unit; citation_author=Mitchell, R.; citation_publication_date=2011; citation_journal_title=J. Comput. Math. Sci. Teach.; citation_volume=30; citation_firstpage=271; citation_lastpage=302" data-hid="citation_reference-399300-56"><meta name="citation_reference" content="citation_title=Augmented Reality and Virtual Reality in Education. Myth or Reality?; citation_author=Elmqaddem, N.; citation_publication_date=2019; citation_journal_title=Int. J. Emerg. Technol. Learn. (IJET); citation_volume=14; citation_firstpage=234; citation_lastpage=242; citation_doi=10.3991/ijet.v14i03.9289" data-hid="citation_reference-399300-57"><meta name="citation_reference" content="citation_title=A Web-based Augmented Reality Plat-form using Pictorial QR Code for Educational Purposes and beyond; citation_author=Nguyen, M., Lai, M.P., Le, H., Yan, W.Q.; citation_publication_date=2019; citation_firstpage=1; citation_lastpage=2" data-hid="citation_reference-399300-59"><meta name="dc.source" content="Applied Sciences 2020, Vol. 10, Page 5660" data-hid="dc.source-399300"><meta name="dc.publisher" content="Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute" data-hid="dc.publisher-399300"><meta name="dc.title" content="Augmented Reality: A Systematic Review of Its Benefits and Challenges in E-learning Contexts" data-hid="dc.title-399300"><meta name="dc.type" content="Review" data-hid="dc.type-399300"><meta name="dc.date" content="2020-08-14" data-hid="dc.date-399300"><meta name="dc.identifier" content="10.3390/app10165660" data-hid="dc.identifier-399300"><meta name="dc.rights" content="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/" data-hid="dc.rights-399300"><meta name="dc.format" content="application/pdf" data-hid="dc.format-399300"><meta name="dc.language" content="en" data-hid="dc.language-399300"><meta name="dc.description" content="Augmented reality (AR) has received increasing attention in the research literature as a fundamental pedagogical tool that can enhance learning at most educational levels. In academic contexts, this technology permits the superimposing of three-dimensional images onto the real environment. Although AR has been found to enhance learning in the academic environment, no systematic review of it has been conducted to identify, evaluate, and summarize empirical findings on its advantages and challenges in e-learning contexts. Hence, a systematic review of the research literature was conducted on the use of AR in e-learning contexts, with a focus on the key benefits and challenges related to its adoption and implementation. Electronic searches on databases, including Springer, Science Direct, EBSCO, and Google Scholar, were performed to retrieve relevant journal articles; 28 studies were included after they were screened using the inclusion and exclusion criteria. The key benefits of using AR in e-learning included support of kinesthetic (tactile) learning, collaborative learning, distance/remote learning, learner-centered learning, and creative learning. Studies also reported that AR enhanced studentsâ€™ engagement, motivation, attention/focus, interactivity, verbal participation, concentration, knowledge retention, and spatial abilities, as well as information accessibility. The findings suggest that challenges associated with AR in e-learning include information and cognitive overload, lack of experience in using the technology, resistance from teachers, complex technology, costly technology, and technical issues, such as connectivity problems." data-hid="dc.descri